  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure to our south moves offshore tonight with \nresulting southwesterly flow warming dewpoints and making for a \nwarmer night than last as temperatures only bottom out in the \nmid to upper 30s. Valley locations stand the best chance of \nseeing temps near or slightly below freezing. The high only \nslowly moves eastward on Monday, so continued southwesterly flow\nis going to allow temperatures to climb into the mid to upper \n60s south of the mountains with 70F possible in the normally \nwarmer southern valleys. Meanwhile at the 500mb level \nnorthwesterly flow and shortwave troughing will add some clouds \nand showers into the mix for the mountains, and temperatures \nlikely only climb into the upper 50s and low 60s as a result.\n\nTuesday stands to be a few degrees cooler across Maine and \neastern New Hampshire as additional high pressure building over \neastern Canada pushes a weak back door cold front through the \narea. This may bring some showers along with it as it slowly \nsinks southward through Tuesday night, but nothing of much \nsubstance, and with plenty of dry air in place over southern \nMaine and New Hampshire it is likely they stay dry. The best \nchance for showers looks to be in Northern New Hampshire and the\nWestern Maine Mountains with QPF only expected to range from a \nfew hundredths to around a tenth of an inch. Any shower activity\nquickly comes to an end by Wednesday morning as the front lifts\nback north. Ridging aloft then continues above normal \ntemperatures and mostly dry weather through Thursday night. \n\nThe is a growing signal for appreciable rain late week as \nglobal models are showing a much better consensus and more \nensemble members are wetter as well. While there is still plenty\nof uncertainty surrounding the details, it is looking more like\nmany locations may see their first measurable rain since mid-\nOctober.",
